{
  "title": "Open Wearables \u2014 healthcare (1,712\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: Open Wearables\nDomain: healthcare | Scale: small | Stars: 1,712\nProblem: Building health apps that support multiple wearable devices requires significant per-provider development effort for OAuth flows, data mapping, and sync logic. Open Wearables provides a unified self-hosted API that normalizes health data from multiple wearable providers so developers can focus on building their product.\nTarget users: Health app developers, Fitness coaching app builders, Healthcare platform engineers, Wellness application developers, Health researchers, Privacy-conscious individuals managing their own wearable data\nCore features: Unified API aggregating data from multiple wearable providers (Garmin, Apple Health, Whoop, Suunto); Normalized health data across devices (heart rate, sleep, activity, steps); Developer portal dashboard with user management and API key creation; Self-hosted deployment via Docker Compose; OAuth flow management per provider handled by the platform; AI-powered health insights and natural language automations; Embeddable widgets for third-party app integration; Webhook notifications for health data events and alerts\nIntegrations: Garmin Connect, Apple Health / Apple Watch, Whoop, Suunto\nTech stack: React with TypeScript, TanStack + Python, FastAPI + PostgreSQL (inferred from Alembic/Mako migrations) + Docker, Docker Compose\nKey libraries: FastAPI, React, TanStack, Alembic\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Provider abstraction pattern is central: a unified normalization layer handles OAuth, data mapping, and sync per wearable provider, shielding consumers from provider-specific complexity.\n- Self-hosting as a first-class design goal drives Docker Compose as the primary deployment method, keeping all infrastructure local and eliminating mandatory third-party cloud dependencies.\n- Separation of developer portal (TypeScript/React frontend) from the API backend (FastAPI) allows independent consumption \u2014 CLI, API clients, and the portal all share one backend surface.\n- AI automations and embeddable widgets are architected as future extension points rather than core functionality, suggesting a deliberate MVP-first delivery strategy.",
