  "metadata": {
    "github_url": "https://github.com/the-momentum/open-wearables",
    "stars": 1712,
    "domain": "healthcare",
    "primary_language": "Python",
    "architecture_pattern": "monolith",
    "tech_stack": {
      "frontend": "React with TypeScript, TanStack",
      "backend": "Python, FastAPI",
      "database": "PostgreSQL (inferred from Alembic/Mako migrations)",
      "infrastructure": "Docker, Docker Compose",
      "key_libraries": [
        "FastAPI",
        "React",
        "TanStack",
        "Alembic"
      ]
    },
    "raw_profile": {
      "project_name": "Open Wearables",
      "problem_statement": "Building health apps that support multiple wearable devices requires significant per-provider development effort for OAuth flows, data mapping, and sync logic. Open Wearables provides a unified self-hosted API that normalizes health data from multiple wearable providers so developers can focus on building their product.",
      "target_users": [
        "Health app developers",
        "Fitness coaching app builders",
        "Healthcare platform engineers",
        "Wellness application developers",
        "Health researchers",
        "Privacy-conscious individuals managing their own wearable data"
      ],
      "core_features": [
        {
          "feature": "Unified API aggregating data from multiple wearable providers (Garmin, Apple Health, Whoop, Suunto)",
          "priority": "must-have"
        },
        {
          "feature": "Normalized health data across devices (heart rate, sleep, activity, steps)",
          "priority": "must-have"
        },
        {
          "feature": "Developer portal dashboard with user management and API key creation",
          "priority": "must-have"
        },
        {
          "feature": "Self-hosted deployment via Docker Compose",
          "priority": "must-have"
        },
        {
          "feature": "OAuth flow management per provider handled by the platform",
          "priority": "must-have"
        },
        {
          "feature": "AI-powered health insights and natural language automations",
          "priority": "nice-to-have"
        },
        {
          "feature": "Embeddable widgets for third-party app integration",
          "priority": "nice-to-have"
        },
        {
          "feature": "Webhook notifications for health data events and alerts",
          "priority": "nice-to-have"
        }
      ],
      "integrations": [
        "Garmin Connect",
        "Apple Health / Apple Watch",
        "Whoop",
        "Suunto"
      ],
      "scale": "small",
      "domain": "healthcare",
      "tech_stack": {
        "frontend": "React with TypeScript, TanStack",
        "backend": "Python, FastAPI",
        "database": "PostgreSQL (inferred from Alembic/Mako migrations)",
        "infrastructure": "Docker, Docker Compose",
        "key_libraries": [
          "FastAPI",
          "React",
          "TanStack",
          "Alembic"
        ]
      },
      "architecture_pattern": "monolith",
      "lessons": [
        "Provider abstraction pattern is central: a unified normalization layer handles OAuth, data mapping, and sync per wearable provider, shielding consumers from provider-specific complexity.",
        "Self-hosting as a first-class design goal drives Docker Compose as the primary deployment method, keeping all infrastructure local and eliminating mandatory third-party cloud dependencies.",
        "Separation of developer portal (TypeScript/React frontend) from the API backend (FastAPI) allows independent consumption \u2014 CLI, API clients, and the portal all share one backend surface.",
        "AI automations and embeddable widgets are architected as future extension points rather than core functionality, suggesting a deliberate MVP-first delivery strategy."
      ]
    }
  }
}